WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.741
Our pipeline is ready to ship: build the image, push it to a registry, an online store for images.

2
00:00:09.741 --> 00:00:11.322
That push needs a key.

3
00:00:11.322 --> 00:00:12.256
So which key?

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.536
Picture the evening our kitchen starts delivering.

5
00:00:15.536 --> 00:00:18.422
The van is outside, and the driver needs a key.

6
00:00:18.422 --> 00:00:22.535
The quick fix: a copy of the master key on a hook by the back door.

7
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.599
It opens the van, the office and the safe.

8
00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:27.217
And it works, for years.

9
00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:31.890
Nothing goes wrong for months, which is exactly why the hook feels fine.

10
00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.109
Then a stranger waiting at the door notices the key.

11
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:37.523
Nobody would ever know a copy was made.

12
00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:44.695
In a pipeline, the master key is a password or a long lived token, stored once and used by every job.

13
00:00:44.695 --> 00:00:49.864
What we really want is a key that opens one door, and expires within hours.

14
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:54.611
A good kitchen gives each cook a key card for one shift.

15
00:00:54.611 --> 00:00:56.052
GitHub does the same.

16
00:00:56.052 --> 00:00:59.896
Every job gets a token without asking: the GITHUB_TOKEN.

17
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.570
GitHub creates it when the job starts.

18
00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.169
It only works on the current repository.

19
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.548
And it expires when the job ends, after six hours at most on GitHub's runners.

20
00:01:10.548 --> 00:01:12.859
A leaked copy is useless by dinner.

21
00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:14.168
Here's the catch.

22
00:01:14.168 --> 00:01:18.830
Every action in the job can use this card, even if you never pass it on.

23
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.068
So whatever the token may do, every action may do.

24
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.001
So you decide what the card opens, with the permissions key.

25
00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:29.887
At the top of the workflow, set a small default: contents read.

26
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.475
Any permission you don't list becomes none.

27
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.912
Then, only in the job that pushes the image, grant one more: packages write.

28
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:40.668
That idea has a name: least privilege.

29
00:01:40.668 --> 00:01:44.269
A card that opens the fridge, but not the cash drawer.

30
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.063
Now the release itself.

31
00:01:47.063 --> 00:01:50.059
A tag is a fixed name for one commit.

32
00:01:50.059 --> 00:01:54.351
You push the tag v1.2.0, and that starts release.yml.

33
00:01:54.567 --> 00:01:57.141
It checks out the code at the tag.

34
00:01:57.141 --> 00:02:02.821
It logs in to GitHub Container Registry, ghcr.io, with the job's own token.

35
00:02:02.821 --> 00:02:05.168
No password is stored anywhere.

36
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:13.407
The metadata action turns the git tag into image tags: 1.2.0, sha plus the short commit, and latest.

37
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:21.555
Then it builds for amd64, most servers, and arm64, like your Mac, and pushes both under one tag.

38
00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:22.914
So far so good.

39
00:02:22.914 --> 00:02:25.209
Now you push a tag by mistake.

40
00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:27.504
Pause and guess: what happens?

41
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:30.947
It ships, at once.

42
00:02:30.947 --> 00:02:32.362
Nobody is in the way.

43
00:02:32.362 --> 00:02:36.879
No restaurant van should leave before the manager checks the order.

44
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.887
So the release job says environment: production.

45
00:02:40.887 --> 00:02:45.131
An environment is a named place you deploy to, with its own rules.

46
00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:50.810
Before the job starts, GitHub checks the environment's protection rules.

47
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.253
A required reviewer: one approval lets the job go on.

48
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:55.617
You click approve.

49
00:02:55.617 --> 00:03:00.859
Only then does the job start, and only then does it get the environment's secrets.

50
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:01.687
One trap.

51
00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:09.751
If a workflow names an environment that doesn't exist yet, GitHub creates it with no rules, and the job runs at once.

52
00:03:09.751 --> 00:03:12.991
Create it, and its rules, before the first tag.

53
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.349
One door is still open.

54
00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:19.759
Every action you call is someone else's code, running with your token.

55
00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:23.096
Say you write uses, some action, at v4.

56
00:03:23.096 --> 00:03:25.021
It feels like a version.

57
00:03:25.021 --> 00:03:26.787
Versions don't change.

58
00:03:26.787 --> 00:03:27.268
Right?

59
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:28.949
A tag is only a label.

60
00:03:28.949 --> 00:03:33.097
Whoever controls that repository can move it to different code.

61
00:03:33.097 --> 00:03:38.101
It's a jar labelled tomato sauce that the supplier can refill with anything.

62
00:03:38.933 --> 00:03:40.225
And it happened.

63
00:03:40.225 --> 00:03:52.088
In March 2025, the tags of the popular tj-actions/changed-files action were moved to a malicious commit that printed CI secrets into workflow logs.

64
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.225
Same label.

65
00:03:53.225 --> 00:03:53.982
New code.

66
00:03:53.982 --> 00:03:56.757
Your workflow file never changed.

67
00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:01.721
A full commit SHA, the 40 character ID of one commit, cannot be moved.

68
00:04:01.721 --> 00:04:09.396
So pin third party actions to one, and keep the version in a comment, the way the lab pins Docker's login action.

69
00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.869
Two guardrails, because this is easy to get almost right.

70
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:14.381
One.

71
00:04:14.381 --> 00:04:15.363
Pins go stale.

72
00:04:15.363 --> 00:04:20.205
Dependabot, GitHub's update helper, opens pull requests to move them.

73
00:04:20.205 --> 00:04:25.608
The lab checks weekly, and only suggests a release once it is seven days old.

74
00:04:25.608 --> 00:04:28.555
The world gets a week to notice a bad one.

75
00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:29.059
Two.

76
00:04:29.059 --> 00:04:34.762
The runner hides secret values in logs as three stars, but only exact matches.

77
00:04:34.762 --> 00:04:39.733
A secret your script changes, say encoded in base64, prints in full.

78
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:43.535
So never echo a secret, and if one leaks, rotate it.

79
00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:45.427
So, the moral.

80
00:04:45.427 --> 00:04:47.211
No master key on the hook.

81
00:04:47.211 --> 00:04:51.397
A key card for one shift, that opens only the doors it needs.

82
00:04:51.397 --> 00:04:52.838
A person at the gate.

83
00:04:52.838 --> 00:04:56.063
And borrowed code locked to a commit you chose.

84
00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:57.516
A tag can move.

85
00:04:57.516 --> 00:04:59.181
A commit SHA cannot.

86
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.270
Quick check.

87
00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.055
An older repository has a workflow with no permissions key at all.

88
00:05:05.055 --> 00:05:06.651
What can its token do?

89
00:05:06.651 --> 00:05:07.811
Pause and think.

90
00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:11.393
Maybe read and write.

91
00:05:11.393 --> 00:05:15.665
Older repositories, and some organizations, still default to that.

92
00:05:15.665 --> 00:05:19.419
So write permissions in every workflow, and stop guessing.
